Title:
Shift Happens – Uncovering Two Built-in Command Injections in Windows Context Menus

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post analyzes two command injection issues in the built-in Windows Explorer “Open PowerShell window here” (Shift + Right-Click) context menu. The root cause is unsafe command-line templating in registry-backed context menu handlers where the current directory path (`%V`) is inserted into a `powershell.exe -Command ...` string without robust quoting/sanitization. By placing PowerShell metacharacters into a folder name (e.g., `folder; calc` or quote-breaking payloads), an attacker can cause arbitrary PowerShell command execution when a user invokes the context menu entry. One variant impacts newer Windows 11 builds due to double-quote handling, while an older variant existed since Windows 10 1703 due to single-quoted `%V` usage and can be exploited by breaking out of the quoted string. The author details reproduction, registry locations, PowerShell argument parsing behavior, NTFS filename character constraints, and practical social-engineering delivery scenarios (ZIP/shortcut/path hiding). It’s useful for red teams and security researchers assessing Windows UI-driven execution paths, and for defenders auditing shell extensions/context menu command templates.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Explorer context menu internals and registry command templates (`HKCR\Directory\shell\...\command`)
- PowerShell `-Command` argument parsing and command delimiter injection (`;`)
- Quoting bugs (single-quote vs double-quote) and string breakout payloads
- NTFS allowed character set in directory names and payload construction constraints
- Exploitation via user interaction (Shift+Right-Click) and path/shortcut-based lures
- Responsible disclosure notes and build/version impact tracking

Use Cases:
- Crafting UI-assisted code execution chains via malicious folder names in delivered archives/projects
- Auditing enterprise images for vulnerable context menu command templates and unsafe quoting patterns
- Building detections for suspicious folder names and Explorer-launched PowerShell with `Set-Location` patterns
- Reproducing and validating Windows build-specific behavior changes for regression/security testing

Keywords:
Windows Explorer, context menu, extended context menu, Shift+Right-Click, Open PowerShell window here, command injection, parameter injection, PowerShell -Command, Set-Location, -LiteralPath, registry HKCR, HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT\Directory\shell\Powershell\command, %V, quoting, single quotes, double quotes, NTFS filename characters, social engineering, MSRC, Windows 10 1703, Windows 11 Canary